              <p class="text-[#16A34A] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">How-To</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">How to Apply Paver Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">Getting the reseal right after all that work.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Chemical stripping is the method that works. Pressure washing alone will not shift a cured sealer film and will empty your joints trying, and mechanical grinding takes paver surface with it. A stripper matched to the sealer chemistry, given its full dwell time, agitated properly and collected with a wet vacuum, is the job.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before you start, confirm you need to. A dull but intact film just needs cleaning and recoating. Fresh haze in a water-based sealer sometimes re-flows with the manufacturer’s solvent. Stripping is for sealer that is peeling, yellowed or clouded through, where a new coat would simply lift along with the old one.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And plan the whole sequence, not just the stripping. You will likely be re-sanding joints afterwards, which means strip, clean, dry thoroughly, re-sand, let the polymeric sand cure for about 30 days, then reseal. Compressing that last interval after all the effort is how people end up with a freshly stripped patio that is cloudy again within a month.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Finally, treat stripping as a warm-season job. Strippers behave unpredictably in the cold, and everything that follows — drying, re-sanding, the polymeric sand cure, the reseal — needs warmth too. Starting in October generally means finishing in May.
          </p>
        </div>


